index e8afe5a..8984cb2 100644
--- a/docs/configuration.md
+++ b/docs/configuration.md
@@ -837,15 +837,46 @@ Manage records without starting a model, from the project directory:
 
 ```sh
 kiln memory export                 # JSON, including correction history
+kiln memory import backup.json     # restore an export into this project
 kiln memory forget test-runner     # deletes every version of this key
 kiln memory restore 12             # restores this project's version as a new record
 ```
 
+`import` reads one JSON array in the `export` format from a file and writes it
+into the current project scope (or the global preferences scope with
+`kiln memory global import`). It never starts a model and never trusts the
+scope, timestamps, or lineage stored in the file: each key's current version is
+the largest input id, assigned a fresh destination id and creation time while
+preserving its expiry. Project file sources are rebound from their recorded
+root into the current project root and re-verified by SHA-256 against the
+destination files; a missing, changed, oversized, non-regular, or escaping
+source rejects the whole import. The global scope accepts preferences without
+sources only.
+
+A conflict is any existing active key in the destination. `--on-conflict`
+chooses how to resolve one (default `error`):
+
+```sh
+kiln memory import backup.json --on-conflict error    # refuse if any key exists (default)
+kiln memory import backup.json --on-conflict skip      # keep existing keys, import the rest
+kiln memory import backup.json --on-conflict replace   # supersede existing keys, preserving history
+kiln memory import backup.json --dry-run               # validate and report, write nothing
+```
+
+Import applies the whole selected batch in one transaction: a validation,
+cancellation, or write failure leaves every memory, search entry, and source
+binding unchanged. On success it prints one JSON object with integer
+`inserted`, `replaced`, and `skipped` counts (for the selected current keys, not
+historical rows) and a boolean `dry_run`; a dry run reports what would happen
+without writing. Inputs are bounded: at most 4 MiB and 1000 records, exactly one
+JSON array, no trailing content, and no unknown fields.
+
 Explicit user preferences can be shared across projects:
 
 ```sh
 kiln memory global remember response-style "Prefer concise answers"
 kiln memory global export
+kiln memory global import prefs.json
 kiln memory global forget response-style
 kiln memory global restore 12
 ```
